> I like this idea. Keep the entire game state on the server, and just send
> the client what it needs to know to update the UI. As long as it's not too
> hard to set it up for an entirely local game, no problems here.

The client knows everything the player knows or could know without 
spending a turn. No latency on far look. Only changes to this knowledge 
are transmitted, over TCP for reliability. These same updates can be 
sent to spectators or to a file, so passive watchers can still check 
inventory or use far look even if the player doesn't.

One problem: the client could be reprogrammed to prevent a wide variety 
of mistakes. For example, it could prevent a player picking up a 
cockatrice corpse when not wearing gloves. This is a problem when 
playing on public servers, especially for a Tournament. Perhaps a closed 
source anti cheat system would be needed to limit players to approved 
clients. Otherwise, the only solution could be to make all clients hold 
the players' hands, thus changing the nature of the game. Or removing 
rubber chickens.

